A good studio gives every useful thing a place without asking everything to
look alike. This note keeps the same words while its surroundings change:
painted angles, bright play structures, or a quiet sky.

## The morning table

Mara opened the windows and set a small notebook beside the lamp. The first
task was simple: find enough room to compare yesterday's sketches. A strong
color could guide the eye, but the pencil marks still needed to be readable.

- Put the three promising sketches beside each other.
- Keep the materials list close to the working notes.
- Leave one corner empty for an unexpected idea.

> Give the work a little space, and it will tell you what matters next.

## What stays dependable

The [studio inventory](./structured.record.card) belongs within easy reach.
Links should look like links, headings should make scanning easier, and a
small screen should preserve the same calm reading order as a large one.

By lunch, the table held fewer things and more possibilities. Nothing valuable
had disappeared. The useful pieces simply had clearer edges, and the next
decision was easier to see.
